title: 9. Interfaces and generics
nav: Learn
order: 900
template: docs
---
<p>An interface lists the members a type promises to have, and generic code uses that list to work on any type without knowing which one it has. You need structs (<a href="book-05-structs.html">chapter 5</a>), unions (<a href="book-06-unions.html">chapter 6</a>), spans (<a href="book-07-collections.html">chapter 7</a>) and error sets (<a href="book-08-errors.html">chapter 8</a>).</p>

<h2 id="interface">An interface and its conformers</h2>
<p>An interface name starts with <code>I</code> and lists bodiless methods. A struct names its interface after a colon.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IShape {
    i64 area()
}

struct Square : IShape {
    i64 side
}</code></pre>
<p>The promise is checked at the struct, not at its use: leave out <code>area</code> and the compiler says what to add.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2801:</span> `Square` does not provide `area` required by `IShape`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> n8.byn:5:17

<span class="gut">  5 │</span> struct Square : IShape {
<span class="gut">    │</span>                 <span class="hl">^^^^^^ needs `area`</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> add `i64 area()` to `Square`
<span class="gut">    │</span> 7 │     i64 area() =&gt; todo("area")
<span class="gut">    │   │</span> <span class="add">++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span> 8 │ }

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2801</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>Conformance is explicit: a struct with an <code>area</code> method does not follow <code>IShape</code> until it says so.</p>

<h2 id="defaults">Default methods and properties</h2>
<p>An interface method may have a body every conformer gets for free and may replace. A bare <code>Type name</code> member is a property conformers must provide, as a field or a computed property, and it may default too.</p>
<example name="book-09-interface"/>
<p><code>IShape</code> requires <code>area</code>, defaults <code>name</code> to <code>"shape"</code> and builds <code>describe</code> on both. <code>Square</code> replaces <code>name</code> and prints <code>square with area 9</code>; <code>Rect</code> keeps the default and prints <code>shape with area 10</code>. The last line is <code>9 + 10</code>.</p>

<h2 id="constraints">Constraints on generic functions</h2>
<p>A type parameter in angle brackets lets a function work on any type, but alone it can do almost nothing with the value. A <code>where</code> clause names the interface the type must follow, making its members available.</p>
<example name="book-09-constraint"/>
<p><code>show</code> and <code>totalArea</code> call <code>describe</code> and <code>area</code> on a <code>T</code>. The compiler builds a copy of each function per type you call it with, so run time pays nothing. <code>totalArea</code> takes a span of <code>T</code>, so one call sums a list of squares; <code>larger</code> takes both values with <code>move</code> and returns one, for the reason below.</p>

<h3 id="constraint-errors">Two mistakes with constraints</h3>
<p>The two mistakes are refused in different places:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Refused at</th></tr>
<tr><td>a member the constraint does not list</td><td>inside the generic function</td></tr>
<tr><td>a type that does not follow the interface</td><td>the call</td></tr>
</table>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 measure&lt;T&gt;(T shape) where T : IShape =&gt; shape.grow()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2806:</span> `T` has no member `grow` in its constraints (`IShape`)
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> n3.byn:12:51

<span class="gut">  12 │</span> i64 measure&lt;T&gt;(T shape) where T : IShape =&gt; shape.grow()
<span class="gut">     │</span>                                                   <span class="hl">^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> add an interface declaring `grow` to the `where` clause

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2806</span></pre>
</figure>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2807:</span> `Square` does not conform to `IShape`: missing `area`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> n2.byn:10:14

<span class="gut">  10 │</span>     print($"{measure(Square { side: 2 })}")
<span class="gut">     │</span>              <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the generic code needs this of its type arguments</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> add `: IShape` to `Square` and provide `area`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2807</span></pre>
</figure>

<h3 id="unique">Generic code and unique values</h3>
<p>A type parameter may stand for a non-copyable type such as a <code>List</code> from <a href="book-07-collections.html">chapter 7</a>, so generic code may not copy a <code>T</code> unless you say how.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">T firstOf&lt;T&gt;(ReadOnlySpan&lt;T&gt; xs) =&gt; xs[0]</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2780:</span> implicit copy of a unique value: type parameter `T` may stand for a unique type
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> n6.byn:1:37

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> T firstOf&lt;T&gt;(ReadOnlySpan&lt;T&gt; xs) =&gt; xs[0]
<span class="gut">    │</span>                                     <span class="hl">^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> pass it by `ref` to share it instead

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2780</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>That is why <code>larger</code> takes its arguments with <code>move</code>: it hands one back, so it must own it.</p>

<h2 id="dyn">Interfaces as parameter types</h2>
<p>An interface name cannot go where a type goes.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2816:</span> `IShape` is an interface, not a type
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> n4.byn:4:13

<span class="gut">  4 │</span> i64 measure(IShape shape) =&gt; shape.area()
<span class="gut">    │</span>             <span class="hl">^^^^^^ an interface</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> write `dyn ref IShape` or add a `where` constraint

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2816</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>Two ways out: a <code>where</code> constraint, or <code>dyn</code>, one compiled function that accepts any conformer and finds its method at run time.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Receiver</th><th>Can do</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>dyn ref readonly</code></td><td>read only</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>dyn ref</code></td><td>call methods that change the value</td></tr>
</table>
<example name="book-09-dyn"/>
<p><code>report</code> works on a <code>Square</code> and a <code>Rect</code>. <code>tick</code> calls <code>bump</code> twice: <code>Clicks</code> adds 1 each time, <code>Steps</code> adds 10, giving <code>2</code> and <code>20</code>.</p>
<p>The receiver keeps its mode: a method taking <code>ref this</code> cannot be called through <code>dyn ref readonly</code>.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2813:</span> `bump` takes `ref this`, but the receiver is `dyn ref readonly ICounter`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> n7.byn:12:7

<span class="gut">  12 │</span>     c.bump()
<span class="gut">     │</span>       <span class="hl">^^^^ declared `ref this`</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> take `dyn ref ICounter`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2813</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>Prefer a constraint, resolved at compile time; use <code>dyn</code> when one function must handle values of different types in one body.</p>
<p>A list of different shapes is unavailable: the type argument is still an interface name.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2816:</span> `IShape` is an interface, not a type
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> n5.byn:9:22

<span class="gut">  9 │</span>     let shapes: List&lt;IShape&gt; = []
<span class="gut">    │</span>                      <span class="hl">^^^^^^ an interface</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> write `dyn ref IShape` or add a `where` constraint

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2816</span></pre>
</figure>

<h2 id="generic-types">Generic structs and unions</h2>
<p>Structs and unions take type parameters too; a method of a generic struct may return it with the parameters reordered.</p>
<example name="book-09-types"/>
<p>The first line shows <code>Pair&lt;i64, string&gt;</code> before and after <code>swapped</code>. The second uses a union with a payload: <code>wrap("hi")</code> infers <code>Option&lt;string&gt;</code> from its argument, while <code>.nothing</code> has nothing to infer from, so <code>let b: Option&lt;i64&gt;</code> names the type. A call with nothing to infer from takes its type in angle brackets, as in <code>same&lt;i64&gt;(2, as: 3)</code>.</p>

<h2 id="fails">Interface methods that can fail</h2>
<p>An interface method lists the error sets it may fail with, like any function, and every implementation must match; the caller sees that set whichever type is behind the <code>dyn</code>.</p>
<example name="book-09-fails"/>
<p><code>Digits.parse</code> fails with <code>empty</code> for <code>""</code> and <code>notDigit</code> for <code>"x"</code>. <code>attempt</code> knows only <code>IParser</code>, yet its <code>catch switch</code> must name both cases. The three lines are <code>7 gives 17</code> (base 10 plus 7), then two fallbacks; the middle line starts empty because the input is empty.</p>
<p>An allocating implementation must say <code>uses alloc</code> on the interface method. Chapter 14 covers effects.</p>

<h2 id="iterate">Making your own type work with <code>for</code></h2>
<p>Two built-in interfaces let <code>for</code> walk your types, and each names its element type with <code>type Item = …</code>:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Interface</th><th>Needs</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>IIndexable</code></td><td><code>count</code> and <code>get</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>IEnumerator</code></td><td><code>moveNext(ref this)</code> and <code>current</code></td></tr>
</table>
<example name="book-09-iterate"/>
<p><code>Tens</code> has no storage: <code>get</code> computes each element, so the loop prints 0, 10, 20 and 30. <code>Down</code> is an enumerator: <code>moveNext</code> steps and reports whether a next value exists; the loop reads <code>current</code> each time and prints 2, 1, 0. An enumerator owns its data, cannot borrow from another collection, so to walk a collection of your own make it an <code>IIndexable</code>.</p>

<h2 id="planned">What is planned</h2>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet. Retroactive conformance, <code>static abstract</code> members, generic methods through an interface constraint, and generic aliases are in the specification; the compiler rejects each.</note>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IParse&lt;TSelf&gt; where TSelf : IParse&lt;TSelf&gt; {
    static abstract TSelf parse(string s) fails ParseError
}

extension Money : IJsonEncode

type Predicate&lt;T&gt; = T -&gt; bool</code></pre>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>Why does <code>book-09-interface</code> print <code>shape with area 10</code> for the <code>Rect</code>?</summary>
<p><code>Rect</code> provides no <code>name</code>, so the default <code>"shape"</code> applies; <code>describe</code> is default too, and leaving a defaulted member out is not an error. The 10 comes from <code>w * h</code> with 2 and 5.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>You write <code>i64 measure(IShape shape)</code>. What does the compiler say, and what are your options?</summary>
<p>BYN2816: an interface is not a type. Write <code>measure&lt;T&gt;(T shape) where T : IShape</code> for a compile-time copy per type, or <code>measure(dyn ref readonly IShape shape)</code> for one function that finds the method at run time.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>What does <code>book-09-dyn</code> print on its last line, and why not <code>2 2</code>?</summary>
<p><code>2 20</code>. <code>tick</code> calls <code>bump</code> twice on each value: <code>Clicks.bump</code> adds 1, <code>Steps.bump</code> adds 10, and the call reaches the real type's method. Both change in place: <code>tick</code> takes <code>dyn ref</code>, not a copy.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>A <code>Square</code> has an <code>area</code> method but its declaration is <code>struct Square</code>, with no <code>: IShape</code>. Can you pass it to <code>show&lt;T&gt;(T) where T : IShape</code>?</summary>
<p>No: conformance is explicit, so the call is refused (BYN2807) with help to add <code>: IShape</code>; the right methods alone are not enough.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>In <code>book-09-types</code>, why does <code>let b: Option&lt;i64&gt; = .nothing</code> need the type written out?</summary>
<p><code>.nothing</code> has no payload, so nothing says what <code>T</code> is; the leading dot also needs an expected type to look <code>nothing</code> up in, and the annotation gives both. <code>wrap("hi")</code> infers from its string argument.</p></details>

